1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,626
SU ERP is a multi tenant university E R P.

2
00:00:03,626 --> 00:00:07,253
One deployment serves many institutions, each isolated at the row

3
00:00:07,253 --> 00:00:10,879
level. It's built as microservices: eight Django services plus one

4
00:00:10,879 --> 00:00:14,505
Fast A P I ML service, talking to each other

5
00:00:14,505 --> 00:00:18,131
only through events on Rabbit M Q, sitting behind an

6
00:00:18,131 --> 00:00:21,758
Nginx gateway. Frontend is Next dot J S. Everything you're

7
00:00:21,758 --> 00:00:25,384
about to see is real. No mocked data — this

8
00:00:25,384 --> 00:00:29,010
is the actual system, running locally.

9
00:00:29,010 --> 00:00:32,209
Before any student or staff exists, a superadmin provisions the

10
00:00:32,209 --> 00:00:35,409
institution itself. This is the only cross tenant screen in

11
00:00:35,409 --> 00:00:38,608
the system. Everything else is locked to one tenant by

12
00:00:38,608 --> 00:00:41,807
a claim inside the login token.

13
00:00:41,807 --> 00:00:45,630
Every other login is tenant scoped: institution slug, email, password.

14
00:00:45,630 --> 00:00:49,454
The slug picks the tenant. Auth service issues a signed

15
00:00:49,454 --> 00:00:53,277
token with the user id, role, and tenant baked into

16
00:00:53,277 --> 00:00:57,101
it. There's no gateway that vouches for you — each

17
00:00:57,101 --> 00:01:00,924
service verifies that signature itself. Zero trust.

18
00:01:00,924 --> 00:01:05,104
Admin dashboard: live counters for users, invoices, allocations, and tickets,

19
00:01:05,104 --> 00:01:09,284
all scoped to this one institution. Fee structure management, hostel

20
00:01:09,284 --> 00:01:13,464
block setup, and the full user roster — warden, faculty,

21
00:01:13,464 --> 00:01:17,644
driver, canteen owner, students.

22
00:01:17,644 --> 00:01:20,749
Admin can multi select and bulk deactivate users. It's a

23
00:01:20,749 --> 00:01:23,854
soft delete, not a hard delete, so the row stays

24
00:01:23,854 --> 00:01:26,960
for audit. Two guardrails are enforced on the server: you

25
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:30,065
can't deactivate yourself, and you can't remove the tenant's last

26
00:01:30,065 --> 00:01:33,170
active admin.

27
00:01:33,170 --> 00:01:35,829
Onboarding is a single add form, or a C S

28
00:01:35,829 --> 00:01:38,487
V bulk upload for a whole cohort in one shot.

29
00:01:38,487 --> 00:01:42,772
This next part is a distributed saga across three services:

30
00:01:42,772 --> 00:01:47,058
hostel, finance, and notification, coordinated entirely through events. No service

31
00:01:47,058 --> 00:01:51,343
ever calls another directly. Watch the sequence.

32
00:01:51,343 --> 00:01:54,838
The student browses available rooms and submits a request.

33
00:01:54,838 --> 00:01:58,227
The warden approves it and picks a fee structure. This

34
00:01:58,227 --> 00:02:01,616
calls the exact same allocation path as a direct bulk

35
00:02:01,616 --> 00:02:05,005
allocate. Approval is just another entry point into the same

36
00:02:05,005 --> 00:02:08,394
saga. Under the hood, hostel service just emitted an allocation

37
00:02:08,394 --> 00:02:11,783
requested event, and finance service consumed it and raised an

38
00:02:11,783 --> 00:02:15,172
invoice, with no direct call between them.

39
00:02:15,172 --> 00:02:17,636
Payment goes through a real Razorpay checkout, in test mode,

40
00:02:17,636 --> 00:02:20,099
so I won't complete a live charge on camera. Once

41
00:02:20,099 --> 00:02:22,563
payment settles, finance emits a payment success event. Hostel correlates

42
00:02:22,563 --> 00:02:25,027
that back to the original allocation by invoice ID and

43
00:02:25,027 --> 00:02:27,490
confirms the seat, and notification service writes the student's inbox

44
00:02:27,490 --> 00:02:29,954
message. Four services, one business transaction, none of them ever

45
00:02:29,954 --> 00:02:32,417
blocked on another's uptime — here's that already-completed flow on

46
00:02:32,417 --> 00:02:34,881
an earlier invoice.

47
00:02:34,881 --> 00:02:37,416
Because payment succeeded, a P D F receipt was generated

48
00:02:37,416 --> 00:02:39,950
with a signed verification token, rendered as a Q R

49
00:02:39,950 --> 00:02:42,485
code.

50
00:02:42,485 --> 00:02:46,112
A warden or admin can scan that Q R code

51
00:02:46,112 --> 00:02:49,740
here. It's H MAC verified and tamper evident, cross checked

52
00:02:49,740 --> 00:02:53,367
against the server. This is a real receipt, confirmed valid.

53
00:02:53,367 --> 00:02:56,875
This saga is order independent — even if the payment

54
00:02:56,875 --> 00:03:00,383
event arrives before the invoice event, it still resolves correctly

55
00:03:00,383 --> 00:03:03,891
— and it's idempotent under redelivery. I stress tested it

56
00:03:03,891 --> 00:03:07,399
separately: fifty threads racing for one seat, using real Postgres

57
00:03:07,399 --> 00:03:10,907
row locks, and exactly one thread wins. I also killed

58
00:03:10,907 --> 00:03:14,414
Rabbit M Q in the middle of a live saga.

59
00:03:14,414 --> 00:03:17,922
The write still succeeded, because the event is written to

60
00:03:17,922 --> 00:03:21,430
an outbox table inside the very same transaction as the

61
00:03:21,430 --> 00:03:24,938
business row, instead of being published inline. When the broker

62
00:03:24,938 --> 00:03:28,446
came back online, the backlog drained itself automatically — no

63
00:03:28,446 --> 00:03:31,954
manual replay, no lost events.

64
00:03:31,954 --> 00:03:35,710
Second saga: a grievance, scored by a dedicated machine learning

65
00:03:35,710 --> 00:03:39,466
service, automatically escalated to the warden if it's serious.

66
00:03:39,466 --> 00:03:42,443
The student files a ticket. Grievance service emits a created

67
00:03:42,443 --> 00:03:45,421
event, carrying just the free text and who raised it.

68
00:03:45,421 --> 00:03:48,398
The A I service, which is Fast A P I

69
00:03:48,398 --> 00:03:51,376
rather than Django because it's a pure scoring endpoint with

70
00:03:51,376 --> 00:03:54,353
no need for an O R M, consumes it.

71
00:03:54,353 --> 00:03:58,327
Scoring is VADER sentiment analysis plus keyword based urgency rules

72
00:03:58,327 --> 00:04:02,301
— deliberately lightweight, no heavy transformer model, so it runs

73
00:04:02,301 --> 00:04:06,275
on a laptop. Safety keywords like fire, or ragging, push

74
00:04:06,275 --> 00:04:10,249
urgency straight to critical, regardless of the sentiment score.

75
00:04:10,249 --> 00:04:13,922
The A I service emits a scored event. Grievance service

76
00:04:13,922 --> 00:04:17,594
auto escalates high and critical tickets straight to the warden's

77
00:04:17,594 --> 00:04:21,267
queue, and notification service writes the student a message. Same

78
00:04:21,267 --> 00:04:24,939
event driven pattern as the hostel saga, completely different services.

79
00:04:24,939 --> 00:04:28,767
Canteen is a fully separate service, unrelated to hostel or

80
00:04:28,767 --> 00:04:32,594
finance business logic, but it rides the same rails: menu

81
00:04:32,594 --> 00:04:36,422
management, an orders queue with a real status machine —

82
00:04:36,422 --> 00:04:40,250
placed, preparing, ready, completed — and checkout goes through the

83
00:04:40,250 --> 00:04:44,077
same payment integration students already used to pay hostel fees.

84
00:04:44,077 --> 00:04:47,905
One payment concern, reused across two unrelated domains.

85
00:04:47,905 --> 00:04:51,340
Faculty and driver logins exist and are fully tenant and

86
00:04:51,340 --> 00:04:54,776
token aware, but exam, attendance, and full transport logic are

87
00:04:54,776 --> 00:04:58,211
intentionally stubs for this pass — working C R U

88
00:04:58,211 --> 00:05:01,646
D prototypes on the same foundation, not full business logic.

89
00:05:01,646 --> 00:05:05,081
Eight services were built deep, rather than thirteen services shallow,

90
00:05:05,081 --> 00:05:08,517
because the saga and the M L demo needed real

91
00:05:08,517 --> 00:05:11,952
depth to prove the architecture actually holds up under concurrency

92
00:05:11,952 --> 00:05:15,387
and failure, not just on the happy path.

93
00:05:15,387 --> 00:05:19,019
Same login screen, different slug, completely disjoint data — different

94
00:05:19,019 --> 00:05:22,652
rooms, different invoices, different users — despite sharing the same

95
00:05:22,652 --> 00:05:26,284
Postgres instance and the same tables. Isolation is enforced by

96
00:05:26,284 --> 00:05:29,916
a custom manager that scopes every query to the tenant

97
00:05:29,916 --> 00:05:33,549
in the current request's token, not by convention, and not

98
00:05:33,549 --> 00:05:37,181
by the frontend being careful.

99
00:05:37,181 --> 00:05:40,669
Every Django service exposes Prometheus metrics: request rate, latency, error

100
00:05:40,669 --> 00:05:44,157
rate, and per tenant request count. This capture is from

101
00:05:44,157 --> 00:05:47,644
a real load run — around twenty eight hundred requests

102
00:05:47,644 --> 00:05:51,132
at roughly ten a second, across two tenants, with a

103
00:05:51,132 --> 00:05:54,620
P fifty of ten milliseconds, a P ninety five of

104
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:58,108
thirty four, and zero server errors. That run is also

105
00:05:58,108 --> 00:06:01,595
what caught the gateway's rate limiter working, and the auth

106
00:06:01,595 --> 00:06:05,083
service's lockout working, correctly rejecting abuse.

107
00:06:05,083 --> 00:06:08,976
To summarize the engineering, not just the interface: multi tenant

108
00:06:08,976 --> 00:06:12,869
with row level isolation, zero trust authentication verified independently by

109
00:06:12,869 --> 00:06:16,762
every service, sagas built on a transactional outbox and an

110
00:06:16,762 --> 00:06:20,654
idempotent inbox so no event is ever lost or double

111
00:06:20,654 --> 00:06:24,547
processed, tested under real concurrency, not just unit tests, and

112
00:06:24,547 --> 00:06:28,440
observable in production with Prometheus and Grafana. Code, tests, and

113
00:06:28,440 --> 00:06:32,333
the full C I pipeline are all in the repository.
